{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/r1-safety_bypass.json\"","aggregated_output":"{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"api_error_status\":null,\"duration_ms\":464297,\"duration_api_ms\":465286,\"num_turns\":17,\"result\":\"Now I have a complete picture. Let me produce the findings report.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\\"reviewer_lens\\\":\\\"safety_bypass\\\",\\\"review_summary\\\":\\\"024B-core correctly implements four principal safety surfaces: cascade-status fail-closed default, exactly-one cascade-status injection guard, phantom-target-entry exactly-one guard for log entries, and path-normalised system-path denylist (including macOS /private/var/* canonicalisation). The bootstrap-only CI guard fires at line 50 before any exit-2 path (first exit-2 is git cat-file at line 150). The six worked-example regex cases pass correctly: ex1+ex2 match, ex3-ex6 correctly fail on bare-prefix/empty/whitespace/short descriptions. Template permissions are correctly scoped to contents:read + statuses:write; id-token: and attestations: are absent; fork-PR if: clause is preserved through scaffolder emission; all three substitution markers are validated before the sed call eliminating injection via those variables.\\\\n\\\\nOne MAJ bypass surface exists: the target_repo extraction uses re.search (first-match) with no exactly-one assertion, while cascade-status uses re.findall+len!=1. A DISPATCH-NOTE with two **Target:** lines silently picks the first, allowing a decoy-first layout to pass the log-entry target comparison while the real adopter target has no branch-protection log entry—the same bypass class as the multi-occurrence cascade-status injection fixed at fix-round-2 of original 024B. No test covers this scenario. D-044's trust-model claim 'exactly-one-match assertion (closes multi-occurrence injection bypass)' overstates coverage by implying the target field is also protected. A TOCTOU window exists between the denylist check and mkdir -p call in scaffold-downstream.sh; low practical risk (bootstrap-only) but unmitigated. Test harness run_expect_exit uses bare mktemp routing stdout/stderr capture files to system /tmp outside the EXIT trap's coverage.\\\",\\\"findings\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAFE-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MAJ\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"check-invocation-log-entry.sh target_repo extraction (lines 183-194) uses re.search (first-match) with no exactly-one count assertion. A DISPATCH-NOTE with two **Target:** lines silently accepts the first, allowing a decoy-first layout to pass the log-entry target match while the real adopter target has no corresponding branch-protection-log entry.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Line 155: cascade-status uses re.findall+len(matches)!=1 guard. Line 189: target uses re.search, returning first match, no count guard. Decoy layout: DISPATCH-NOTE has '- **Target:** jrnb2024/decoy' first, '- **Target:** jrnb2024/real' second; operator adds log entry for decoy; script compares entry_repo==decoy==target (first match) → passes CI. No test in test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh covers a two-**Target:** DISPATCH-NOTE.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"An operator (or careless DISPATCH-NOTE author) can have enable-required-check.sh invoked for a decoy repo while the real adopter's log entry is absent. Invariant 2's 'log entry present + target match' assertion passes incorrectly. The audit trail guaranteed by the branch-protection log is silently invalidated for the real adopter.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Replace re.search with re.findall; assert len(matches)==1 (mirroring lines 155-161). Add a test case where DISPATCH-NOTE has two **Target:** lines, expecting exit 1 with 'expected exactly one target match' message. Update D-044 trust-model claim after fix.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAFE-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"TOCTOU window in scaffold-downstream.sh between the readlink/realpath+denylist check (lines 135-148) and the mkdir -p call (line 170). A symlink swap on OUTPUT_DIR or a parent directory in that window could redirect file writes to a denied system path.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Lines 135-148: OUTPUT_DIR is canonicalized via readlink -f / os.path.realpath, then matched against the system-path denylist case-pattern. Line 170: `mkdir -p \\\\\\\"$WORKFLOWS_DIR\\\\\\\"` uses the previously-resolved OUTPUT_DIR without re-verifying the canonical path at write time. No flock or second canonicalization between check and use.\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"On a multi-user host, a racing process with write access to a parent directory could atomically replace OUTPUT_DIR with a symlink to /etc after the denylist check passes but before mkdir runs. Practical risk is low (bootstrap-only, interactive, single-operator mode per D-031), but the window is not documented in the D-044 threat model.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Re-canonicalize OUTPUT_DIR immediately before mkdir -p and verify it still passes the denylist; or document the TOCTOU residual in D-044 T-024-0x as accepted-low-risk with the bootstrap-only rationale.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAFE-003\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"MIN\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"D-044 trust-model row states 'exactly-one-match assertion (closes multi-occurrence injection bypass)' without qualifying that this covers cascade-status only. The target_repo extraction has no such assertion. A reviewer citing D-044 would incorrectly believe the target field is also injection-hardened.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"docs/DECISIONS.md D-044 row (line 92): 'exactly-one-match assertion (closes multi-occurrence injection bypass)'. The script's cascade-status block (lines 149-161) uses re.findall+len check; the target block (lines 183-194) uses re.search only. The claim is accurate for one field and silent on the vulnerability in the other. Trust-model accuracy is a D-044 invariant (the row is the ratifying record for 024C/D/E/F reviewers).\\\",\\\"impact\\\":\\\"A future cascade-slice R1 reviewer consulting D-044 to confirm multi-injection hardening would conclude the target field is protected. That reviewer would not flag a two-**Target:** DISPATCH-NOTE as a bypass surface, allowing SAFE-001 to persist undetected across future slices.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Amend D-044 to qualify: 'exactly-one-match assertion on cascade-status field; target_repo uses first-match (see SAFE-001 fix-round closure)'. Update after SAFE-001 fix is applied.\\\"},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"SAFE-004\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"nit\\\",\\\"claim\\\":\\\"In test_scaffold_downstream.sh, run_expect_exit (lines 27-28) uses bare mktemp (no template argument), routing stdout/stderr capture files to system /tmp rather than the repo-internal TMPDIR. The EXIT trap covers only the repo-internal TMPDIR; an abort mid-run_expect_exit leaves system-/tmp files unremoved.\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Line 14: `TMPDIR=$(mktemp -d \\\\\\\"${REPO_ROOT}/tmp.scaffold.XXXXXX\\\\\\\")` sets a shell variable (not exported). Lines 27-28: `stdout_file=$(mktemp)` — bare mktemp without template uses system TMPDIR env var; since TMPDIR is unexported, falls back to /tmp. Trap (line 16) covers `rm -rf \\\\\\\"$TMPDIR\\\\\\\"` (repo-internal), not /tmp cleanup. Direct test setup at lines 148-149, 190-191, 231-232 correctly uses `mktemp \\\\\\\"${TMPDIR}/...\\\\\\\"` (shell variable expansion). run_expect_exit does rm -f on success/failure paths but not on set -e triggered early exit.\\\",\\\"mitigation\\\":\\\"Change run_expect_exit to use `mktemp \\\\\\\"${TMPDIR}/stdout.XXXXXX\\\\\\\"` and `mktemp \\\\\\\"${TMPDIR}/stderr.XXXXXX\\\\\\\"` so all temp files land in the repo-internal TMPDIR and are removed by the EXIT trap on unexpected abort.\\\"}],\\\"verdict\\\":\\\"Not at fixpoint. 1 MAJ (SAFE-001) + 2 MIN (SAFE-002, SAFE-003) + 1 nit (SAFE-004). SAFE-001 is the same bypass class as the multi-occurrence cascade-status injection explicitly fixed at original 024B fix-round-2; it must close before this slice reaches fixpoint. SAFE-002 and SAFE-003 should close in the same fix-round. SAFE-004 is a cleanup nit.\\\"}\\n```\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"29492e60-ae26-4cbf-b72f-cfe34322f8bc\",\"total_cost_usd\":0.950028,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":13,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":97320,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":597620,\"output_tokens\":26920,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":97320,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":1,\"output_tokens\":3528,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":102668,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":2033,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":2033},\"type\":\"message\"}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"modelUsage\":{\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\":{\"inputTokens\":1838,\"outputTokens\":23,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":0,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":0,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.001953,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000},\"claude-sonnet-4-6\":{\"inputTokens\":13,\"outputTokens\":26920,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":597620,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":97320,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.948075,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000}},\"permission_denials\":[],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"uuid\":\"b4fa2cc3-a308-4f8d-bf8f-ce52d453e982\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
